<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Network Audio Service.app (issue #96 item 4) - the Applications entry that opens the control
  page, and since issue #105 the process the service runs as. SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later

  Its executable is the daemon itself, a second copy of na_audio_daemon placed at
  Contents/MacOS by the postinstall. The launchd agent runs THAT copy, because macOS attributes a
  Microphone request to the process's bundle and to nothing else: run from /usr/local/bin the
  agent showed up in the permission prompt and in Privacy & Security as "na_audio_daemon" with a
  generic icon, and an Info.plist embedded in that executable changed neither (measured
  2026-09-15 on macOS 27). Run from here it is this bundle - the name below, the icon, and
  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription as the sentence in the prompt. A double-click in the Finder runs
  the same executable with no arguments, which the daemon takes as "open the control page"; the
  launcher script that used to pass those arguments is gone. The sentence names the service, not
  the project: "naudio" is the library's name and means nothing to the person reading a prompt
  (operator, 2026-09-15, on seeing the first one).

  The bundle is named after the SERVICE, not the page, because on macOS this bundle is the
  service's identity: Login Items lists the launchd agent under the name of the app the agent
  names through AssociatedBundleIdentifiers (issue #102), and that row is the service's switch.
  CFBundleName, CFBundleDisplayName and the on-disk name the postinstall assembles must all be
  this same string - the Finder shows the on-disk name, and it ignores a CFBundleDisplayName
  that differs from it (measured 2026-09-14: LaunchServices resolved the display name, the
  Finder's own displayName(atPath:) kept the file name, with and without LSHasLocalizedDisplayName
  and a localized InfoPlist.strings). All three come from one CMake variable (tools/CMakeLists.txt,
  NAUDIO_APP_NAME). The identifier is unchanged: it is the key Background Task Management already
  holds for the association, and identifiers are not shown to anyone.

  CFBundleIconFile names the icon the Finder, the Dock, Login Items, the Microphone prompt and the
  "App Background Activity" notification all show for this bundle (issue #103):
  packaging/icons/naudio.icns, carried in the payload beside the other pieces and placed under
  Contents/Resources by the postinstall. The seal covers it, so cmake/CodesignPayload.cmake
  assembles it into the bundle it signs, and the release gate asserts the installed file this key
  names exists.

  LSUIElement is true so this does not take over the Dock and the menu bar as a foreground app
  would; it is a background program whose interface is a web page.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>@NAUDIO_APP_NAME@</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>@NAUDIO_APP_NAME@</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>org.kj5hst.naudio.control</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>@PROJECT_VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>@PROJECT_VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>na_audio_daemon</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>naudio.icns</string>
    <key>LSUIElement</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>11.0</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Network Audio Service streams the audio your radio's USB audio interface receives to your digital-mode software.</string>
    <key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
    <string>Copyright (C) 2025-2026 Terrell Deppe. LGPL-2.1-or-later.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
